    },
    {
      "SourceElementId": "G11-U03-L14-ACTIVITY-004",
      "SourceSection": "14.2: Powers of $i$",
      "SourceExcerpt": "Write each power of \\(i\\) in the form \\(a+bi\\), where \\(a\\) and \\(b\\) are real numbers. If \\(a\\) or \\(b\\) is zero, you can ignore that part of the number. For example, \\(0+3i\\) can simply be expressed as \\(3i\\).",
      "SourceLessonArtifact": "src/Edulytics.Core/Curriculum/LessonContent/RichV2/us-ccss-hs-alg2-u03-l14.rich-lesson-v2.json",
      "Treatment": "SOURCE_SCOPED_AUTHORING_WITH_TEACHER_REVIEW",
      "TestsExecuted": "NOT_RUN"
    },
    {
      "SourceElementId": "G11-U03-L14-ACTIVITY-005",
      "SourceSection": "14.2: Powers of $i$",
      "SourceExcerpt": "What is \\(i^{100}\\)? Explain your reasoning.",
      "SourceLessonArtifact": "src/Edulytics.Core/Curriculum/LessonContent/RichV2/us-ccss-hs-alg2-u03-l14.rich-lesson-v2.json",
      "Treatment": "SOURCE_SCOPED_AUTHORING_WITH_TEACHER_REVIEW",
      "TestsExecuted": "NOT_RUN"
    },
    {
      "SourceElementId": "G11-U03-L14-ACTIVITY-006",
      "SourceSection": "14.2: Powers of $i$",
      "SourceExcerpt": "What is \\(i^{38}\\)? Explain your reasoning.",
      "SourceLessonArtifact": "src/Edulytics.Core/Curriculum/LessonContent/RichV2/us-ccss-hs-alg2-u03-l14.rich-lesson-v2.json",
      "Treatment": "SOURCE_SCOPED_AUTHORING_WITH_TEACHER_REVIEW",
      "TestsExecuted": "NOT_RUN"
    },
    {
      "SourceElementId": "G11-U03-L14-ACTIVITY-007",
      "SourceSection": "14.2: Powers of $i$",
      "SourceExcerpt": "Write each power of \\(1+i\\) in the form \\(a+bi\\), where \\(a\\) and \\(b\\) are real numbers. If \\(a\\) or \\(b\\) is zero, you can ignore that part of the number. For example, \\(0+3i\\) can simply be expressed as \\(3i\\).",
      "SourceLessonArtifact": "src/Edulytics.Core/Curriculum/LessonContent/RichV2/us-ccss-hs-alg2-u03-l14.rich-lesson-v2.json",
      "Treatment": "SOURCE_SCOPED_AUTHORING_WITH_TEACHER_REVIEW",
      "TestsExecuted": "NOT_RUN"
    },
    {
      "SourceElementId": "G11-U03-L14-ACTIVITY-008",
      "SourceSection": "14.2: Powers of $i$",
      "SourceExcerpt": "Compare and contrast the powers of \\(1+i\\) with the powers of \\(i\\). What is the same? What is different?",
      "SourceLessonArtifact": "src/Edulytics.Core/Curriculum/LessonContent/RichV2/us-ccss-hs-alg2-u03-l14.rich-lesson-v2.json",
      "Treatment": "SOURCE_SCOPED_AUTHORING_WITH_TEACHER_REVIEW",
      "TestsExecuted": "NOT_RUN"
    },
    {
      "SourceElementId": "G11-U03-L14-ACTIVITY-009",
      "SourceSection": "14.3: Add 'Em Up (or Subtract or Multiply)",
      "SourceExcerpt": "For each row, your partner and you will each rewrite an expression so it has the form \\(a+bi\\), where \\(a\\) and \\(b\\) are real numbers. You and your partner should get the same answer. If you disagree, work to reach agreement.",
      "SourceLessonArtifact": "src/Edulytics.Core/Curriculum/LessonContent/RichV2/us-ccss-hs-alg2-u03-l14.rich-lesson-v2.json",
      "Treatment": "SOURCE_SCOPED_AUTHORING_WITH_TEACHER_REVIEW",
      "TestsExecuted": "NOT_RUN"
    },
    {
      "SourceElementId": "G11-U03-L14-ACTIVITY-010",
      "SourceSection": "14.3: Add 'Em Up (or Subtract or Multiply)",
      "SourceExcerpt": "Suppose we want to write the product \\((3+5i)(7-2i)\\) in the form \\(a+bi\\), where \\(a\\) and \\(b\\) are real numbers. For example, we might want to compare our solution with a partner’s, and having answers in the same form makes that easier.…",
      "SourceLessonArtifact": "src/Edulytics.Core/Curriculum/LessonContent/RichV2/us-ccss-hs-alg2-u03-l14.rich-lesson-v2.json",
      "Treatment": "SOURCE_SCOPED_AUTHORING_WITH_TEACHER_REVIEW",
      "TestsExecuted": "NOT_RUN"
    },
    {
      "SourceElementId": "G11-U03-L14-ACTIVITY-011",
      "SourceSection": "14.3: Add 'Em Up (or Subtract or Multiply)",
      "SourceExcerpt": "\\(\\displaystyle \\begin{align} (3+5i)(7-2i) &= 21 - 6i +35i - 10i^2 \\\\ &= 21 + 29i - 10(\\text-1) \\\\ &= 21+ 29i +10 \\\\ &= 31 +29i \\end{align}\\)",
      "SourceLessonArtifact": "src/Edulytics.Core/Curriculum/LessonContent/RichV2/us-ccss-hs-alg2-u03-l14.rich-lesson-v2.json",
      "Treatment": "SOURCE_SCOPED_AUTHORING_WITH_TEACHER_REVIEW",
      "TestsExecuted": "NOT_RUN"
    },
    {
      "SourceElementId": "G11-U03-L14-ACTIVITY-012",
      "SourceSection": "14.3: Add 'Em Up (or Subtract or Multiply)",
      "SourceExcerpt": "Keeping track of the negative signs is especially important since it is easy to mix up the fact that \\(i^2=\\text-1\\) with the fact that \\(\\text-i^2=\\text-(\\text-1) = 1\\).",
      "SourceLessonArtifact": "src/Edulytics.Core/Curriculum/LessonContent/RichV2/us-ccss-hs-alg2-u03-l14.rich-lesson-v2.json",
      "Treatment": "SOURCE_SCOPED_AUTHORING_WITH_TEACHER_REVIEW",
      "TestsExecuted": "NOT_RUN"
    },
    {
      "SourceElementId": "G11-U03-L14-ACTIVITY-013",
      "SourceSection": "14.3: Add 'Em Up (or Subtract or Multiply)",
      "SourceExcerpt": "Next, suppose we want to write the difference \\((\\text-6+3i) - (2-4i)\\) as a single complex number in the form \\(a+bi\\). Distributing the negative and combining like terms, we get:",
      "SourceLessonArtifact": "src/Edulytics.Core/Curriculum/LessonContent/RichV2/us-ccss-hs-alg2-u03-l14.rich-lesson-v2.json",
      "Treatment": "SOURCE_SCOPED_AUTHORING_WITH_TEACHER_REVIEW",
      "TestsExecuted": "NOT_RUN"
    },
    {
      "SourceElementId": "G11-U03-L14-ACTIVITY-014",
      "SourceSection": "14.3: Add 'Em Up (or Subtract or Multiply)",
      "SourceExcerpt": "\\(\\displaystyle \\begin{align} (\\text-6+3i) - (2-4i) &= \\text-6 +3i - 2 - (\\text-4i) \\\\ &= \\text-8 +3i +4i \\\\ &= \\text-8 +7i \\end{align}\\)",
      "SourceLessonArtifact": "src/Edulytics.Core/Curriculum/LessonContent/RichV2/us-ccss-hs-alg2-u03-l14.rich-lesson-v2.json",
      "Treatment": "SOURCE_SCOPED_AUTHORING_WITH_TEACHER_REVIEW",
      "TestsExecuted": "NOT_RUN"
    },
    {
      "SourceElementId": "G11-U03-L14-ACTIVITY-015",
      "SourceSection": "14.3: Add 'Em Up (or Subtract or Multiply)",
      "SourceExcerpt": "Again, it is important to be precise with negative signs. It is a common mistake to just subtract \\(4i\\) rather than subtracting \\(\\text-4i\\).",
      "SourceLessonArtifact": "src/Edulytics.Core/Curriculum/LessonContent/RichV2/us-ccss-hs-alg2-u03-l14.rich-lesson-v2.json",
      "Treatment": "SOURCE_SCOPED_AUTHORING_WITH_TEACHER_REVIEW",
      "TestsExecuted": "NOT_RUN"
    },
    {
      "SourceElementId": "G11-U03-L14-ACTIVITY-016",
      "SourceSection": "14.3: Add 'Em Up (or Subtract or Multiply)",
      "SourceExcerpt": "A number in the complex plane. It can be written as \\(a + bi\\), where \\(a\\) and \\(b\\) are real numbers and \\(i^2 = \\text-1\\).",
      "SourceLessonArtifact": "src/Edulytics.Core/Curriculum/LessonContent/RichV2/us-ccss-hs-alg2-u03-l14.rich-lesson-v2.json",
      "Treatment": "SOURCE_SCOPED_AUTHORING_WITH_TEACHER_REVIEW",
      "TestsExecuted": "NOT_RUN"
    },
    {
      "SourceElementId": "G11-U03-L14-ACTIVITY-017",
      "SourceSection": "14.3: Add 'Em Up (or Subtract or Multiply)",
      "SourceExcerpt": "A number on the imaginary number line. It can be written as \\(bi\\), where \\(b\\) is a real number and \\(i^2 = \\text-1\\).",
      "SourceLessonArtifact": "src/Edulytics.Core/Curriculum/LessonContent/RichV2/us-ccss-hs-alg2-u03-l14.rich-lesson-v2.json",
      "Treatment": "SOURCE_SCOPED_AUTHORING_WITH_TEACHER_REVIEW",
      "TestsExecuted": "NOT_RUN"
    },
